Identify **all** GitHub users you may act as. `gh auth status` can list
multiple `github.com` accounts (e.g. a work account and a personal one), but
`--author @me` and `gh api user -q '.login'` only ever resolve to the single
currently-active account — which means PRs authored by any of your other
logged-in accounts are silently invisible.

Enumerate every `github.com` account (active or not) from `gh auth status`:

```bash
gh auth status 2>&1 \
  | awk '
      /^[^ ]/            { host=$1; next }
      /Logged in to/     { if (host=="github.com") { for (i=1;i<=NF;i++) if ($i=="account") print $(i+1) } }
    ' \
  | sort -u
```

Store this as the **owned-logins set** — every login in this set counts as
"you" for the rest of the prompt. Only act on PRs where `author.login` is a
member of this set (rebase, merge, spawn fix conversations). If the set is
empty for any reason, fall back to `gh api user -q '.login'`.
